[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/107757", :title "Claude Code sends your email address to websites", :link "https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/107757", :published-at #inst "2026-11-05T22:17:15.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "Claude Code sends your email address to websites", :matched-terms ["claude"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "rails-deploy", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://dev.to/ganesh_rajendran_art/proving-what-ai-generates-running-formally-verified-lean-4-inside-clojure-services-3hip", :title "Proving What AI Generates: Running Formally Verified Lean 4 Inside Clojure Services", :link "https://dev.to/ganesh_rajendran_art/proving-what-ai-generates-running-formally-verified-lean-4-inside-clojure-services-3hip", :published-at #inst "2026-11-06T04:52:16.000-00:00", :content "TL;DR: Unit tests only check sample points; formal verification in Lean 4 mathematically guarantees logic for all inputs. The new Ansatz Lean/JVM SDK (org.replikativ/ansatz-lean-jvm:0.2.615) lets you compile Lean into an intermediate artifact (.leanjvm) and interpret it directly on standard OpenJDK 17+ with zero native C/JNI dependencies. This article demonstrates how an AI coding agent can write financial ledger invariants, self-correct via Lean compiler proofs, and deploy proved code into a Clojure service. Credit & Attribution: This workflow builds on the breakthrough work by the Simmis team introducing the Ansatz Lean/JVM SDK (org.replikativ/ansatz-lean-jvm). The financial settlement walkthrough below illustrates these mechanics applied to autonomous agent engineering. The LLM Reliability Wall in Production Every engineering team deploying autonomous AI coding agents eventually runs into the LLM Reliability Wall. An agent prompted to generate an account transfer or settlement routine writes clean code in seconds. It even produces dozens of passing unit tests. But unit tests only sample finite scenarios. In financial ledgers, the bug that triggers insolvency is never the one tested by a handful of mock transactions—it lurks in the untested corner case where an overdraft limit, a currency conversion edge, and a zero-sum constraint collide. For decades, formal methods offered the mathematical antidote: prove that code satisfies invariants for all possible inputs using proof assistants like Lean 4. But Lean code was traditionally an island: Native binaries required brittle JNI or Panama bridges. Memory models clashed: Lean frees memory via reference counting, while the JVM uses a garbage collector. Cycles across the boundary leak memory. Deployed servers required the full native Lean toolchain installed. The release of the Ansatz Lean/JVM SDK (org.replikativ/ansatz-lean-jvm:0.2.615) changes this equation. It provides a pure JVM intermediate-representation (IR) interpreter that runs proved Lean functions directly on stock OpenJDK 17+. This unlocks an autonomous ground-truth loop: agents synthesize formally verified invariants in Lean, prove them against the Lean compiler, and deploy them directly into production Clojure and Java microservices. The Architecture: Build-Time Proofs, Run-Time JVM ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                   BUILD & PROOF STAGE                  │\n│               (Linux x86-64 / CI / Docker)             │\n│                                                        │\n│  AI Agent writes:                                      │\n│  • Settlement logic                                    │\n│  • Invariant theorems (Conservation & Solvency)        │\n│                                                        │\n│                     ▼                                  │\n│         Lean 4.33.1 Toolchain Check                    │\n│    [ Fails: Agent self-corrects proof ]                │\n│    [ Passes: Proof erased, IR compiled ]               │\n│                     ▼                                  │\n│            ledger.leanjvm Artifact                     │\n│          + SHA-256 Manifest Fingerprint                │\n└────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┘\n                         │\n                         ▼\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                   PRODUCTION STAGE                     │\n│              (Any OpenJDK 17+ Environment)             │\n│                                                        │\n│  Clojure / JVM Microservice                            │\n│  • Loads ledger.leanjvm into pure Java Interpreter     │\n│  • Zero native C / JNI / platform dependencies         │\n│  • Managed 100% by JVM Garbage Collector               │\n│  • Enforces instruction budgets (default: 2M ops)      │\n│  • Exposes bidirectional host callbacks (IO)           │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n The division of labor is clean: At build time, native Lean compiles the source, checks the proofs, and strips them away. The compiler packages the intermediate code, types, and layouts into a portable .leanjvm artifact. At run time, standard Clojure services interpret the intermediate representation. The production container needs no Lean toolchain. The Domain: A Conservation-of-Value Financial Settlement Consider an account settlement function. The business requires two non-negotiable invariants: Solvency: No account may be debited below its configured floor (zero). Conservation of Value: The total amount debited must equal the total amount credited. No money appears or vanishes. Let's see how an autonomous agent interacts with this system. 1. The Agent Self-Correction Loop When an AI agent is instructed to write settlement logic, it often generates subtle bugs. Here is what happens when Lean's type checker acts as the agent's supervisor. Round 1: The Agent Hallucinates an Edge Case The agent drafts an initial version in Ledger.lean: namespace Ledger\n\ndef transfer (fromBal toBal amount : Nat) : (Nat × Nat) :=\n  -- Naive transfer: subtracts without checking if balance < amount\n  (fromBal - amount, toBal + amount)\n\ntheorem transfer_conserves_value (fromBal toBal amount : Nat) :\n  let (f, t) := transfer fromBal toBal amount\n  f + t = fromBal + toBal := by\n  unfold transfer\n  -- Proof attempt fails when amount > fromBal due to Nat underflow truncation!\n In Lean, natural numbers (Nat) truncate subtraction at zero (3 - 5 = 0). Because of this, fromBal - amount breaks conservation of value whenever amount > fromBal! The Lean compiler rejects the code: error: unsolved goals\ncase h\nfromBal toBal amount : Nat\n⊢ (fromBal - amount) + (toBal + amount) = fromBal + toBal\n The agent cannot hallucinate or hand-wave this failure away. The build stops cold. Round 2: Self-Correction with Machine-Checked Solvency The agent reads the compiler error, realizes Nat subtraction truncates at zero, and refines the logic to guard the transfer: namespace Ledger\n\ndef settleTransfer (fromBal toBal amount : Nat) : (Nat × Nat) :=\n  if amount ≤ fromBal then\n    (fromBal - amount, toBal + amount)\n  else\n    (fromBal, toBal) -- Rejected transfer preserves balances unchanged\n\n/-- Theorem: Transfers strictly conserve total capital. -/\ntheorem settle_conserves_value (fromBal toBal amount : Nat) :\n  let (f, t) := settleTransfer fromBal toBal amount\n  f + t = fromBal + toBal := by\n  unfold settleTransfer\n  split\n  · rename_i h\n    omega\n  · rfl\n\n/-- Theorem: The sender account never drops below zero (solvency guarantee). -/\ntheorem settle_preserves_solvency (fromBal toBal amount : Nat) :\n  let (f, _) := settleTransfer fromBal toBal amount\n  f ≤ fromBal := by\n  unfold settleTransfer\n  split\n  · rename_i h\n    omega\n  · rfl\n\nend Ledger\n Both proofs pass. Lean guarantees that no transaction can ever violate solvency or fabricate currency, across all infinite combinations of Nat. 2. Compiling the Verified Artifact During the CI build (currently supported on Linux x86-64 with prlimit, or via Docker), the agent triggers artifact preparation: java -cp \"$SDK_CLASSPATH\" ansatz.lean.jvm.LeanPreparation \\\n  --source ./ledger \\\n  --sysroot \"$LEAN_SYSROOT\" \\\n  --producer-bundle \"$PRODUCER_BUNDLE\" \\\n  --cache \"$COMPILER_CACHE\" \\\n  --root-module Ledger \\\n  --entry Ledger.settleTransfer \\\n  --output ./ledger.leanjvm\n This generates ledger.leanjvm and prints its SHA-256 fingerprint: sha256:d8a2...3f10 3. Running Inside Clojure at the REPL In the production service, add the SDK dependency to deps.edn: {:deps {org.replikativ/ansatz-lean-jvm {:mvn/version \"0.2.615\"}}}\n Load the artifact and invoke the formally proved function directly from the Clojure REPL: (ns ledger.core\n  (:require [ansatz.lean.jvm :as lean]\n            [clojure.java.io :as io]))\n\n(defn run-settlement []\n  (with-open [runtime (lean/open)\n              input   (io/input-stream \"ledger.leanjvm\")\n              project (lean/load-artifact runtime input (System/getenv \"EXPECTED_MANIFEST_SHA256\"))]\n    (let [settle (lean/function project \"Ledger.settleTransfer\")]\n      ;; 1. Valid transfer: $500 from Acc1 ($10,000) to Acc2 ($1,000)\n      ;; 2. Overdraft attempt: $15,000 from Acc1 ($10,000) to Acc2 ($1,000)\n      {:valid-transfer     (settle 10000 1000 500)\n       :overdraft-rejected (settle 10000 1000 15000)})))\n\n;; (run-settlement)\n;; => {:valid-transfer     [9500N 1500N]\n;;     :overdraft-rejected [10000N 1000N]}\n Key runtime observations: Clojure passes standard numbers and receives back persistent vectors containing java.math.BigInteger instances. Zero JNI code or platform native bridges. Memory management is 100% handled by the JVM garbage collector. 4. Inversion of Control: Lean Calling Clojure Real business operations require dynamic runtime data—like querying fee tiers from a database or configuration file. Lean declares the external callback: namespace Ledger\n\n@[extern \"ansatz_jvm_account_fee\"]\nopaque getAccountFee : String → IO Nat\n\ndef settleWithFee (fromBal toBal amount : Nat) (tier : String) : IO (Nat × Nat) := do\n  let feeRate ← getAccountFee tier\n  let fee := (amount * feeRate) / 1000\n  let totalDebit := amount + fee\n  if totalDebit ≤ fromBal then\n    pure (fromBal - totalDebit, toBal + amount)\n  else\n    pure (fromBal, toBal)\n\nend Ledger\n In Clojure, supply the implementation under the declaration's name when loading the artifact: (def host-bindings\n  {\"Ledger.getAccountFee\"\n   {:implementation-id \"fees/v1\"\n    :effect :io\n    :fn (fn [tier]\n          (case tier\n            \"vip\"      0N     ;; Zero fee\n            \"standard\" 15N    ;; 1.5% fee\n            50N))}})          ;; Default 5.0%\n\n(with-open [runtime (lean/open)\n              input   (io/input-stream \"ledger.leanjvm\")\n              project (lean/load-artifact runtime input \n                                          (System/getenv \"EXPECTED_MANIFEST_SHA256\")\n                                          host-bindings)]\n  ;; IO functions return an IOResult object carrying values or errors\n  (let [result (lean/call project \"Ledger.settleWithFee\" 10000 1000 2000 \"standard\")]\n    (if (.isOk result)\n      (.getValue result)\n      (throw (ex-info \"Lean IO Error\" {:cause (.getError result)})))))\n;; => [7970N 3000N]  ; Total debit: 2000 + 30 fee = 2030\n Mathematical proofs that parameterize over host inputs remain sound, even when values are resolved dynamically by host Clojure code. 5. Architectural Differentiator: Forkable Execution State Why run Lean through a pure JVM interpreter instead of compiling hot machine code? The Simmis team highlighted an architectural breakthrough: execution state forkability. Because Lean's entire execution state (call frames, heap objects, and registers) is stored as standard JVM heap objects, the engine can clone that state mid-computation. Pausing an engine allows branching it into two independent engines that proceed down separate computational paths. Note: While the core Ansatz engine implements state forking internally today, the public SDK does not expose fork handles yet. It represents groundwork for upcoming releases. For future AI reasoning pipelines, this capability enables: Counterfactual Branching: An agent pauses a complex financial settlement, branches the execution into two what-if paths (e.g., assessing different liquidity routing strategies), and compares the outcomes. Rollback without Transaction Resets: Discarding a failed branch requires only dropping the cloned JVM object, with zero external database rollback needed. 6. Safety & Operational Guardrails When executing autonomous agent code in production, defensive constraints are mandatory: Guardrail Enforcement Mechanism Infinite Recursion / DOS Invocations run under strict instruction budgets (default: 2,000,000 steps) and wall-clock timeouts (default: 5.0s). Tampered / Stale Artifacts Artifact loading strictly validates the build-time SHA-256 manifest hash before execution. Error Isolation Out-of-budget calls fail cleanly with ProjectFailed. The service restarts the engine via project.restart(), discarding corrupted state while preserving verified code handles. Language Constraints The current release enforces closed, first-order signatures across host boundaries and caps project modules at 128 (beyond Lean's Init). Conclusion The conversation around AI coding agents has focused primarily on generating code and generating unit tests. But in high-consequence domains like finance, tests cannot establish absolute correctness. By uniting Lean 4's proof assistant with the JVM's operational maturity, tools like Ansatz establish a practical verification bridge: Agents specify and prove invariants rather than guessing implementations. The compiler enforces ground truth, rejecting flawed edge cases with actionable counter-examples. The runtime executes natively on standard JVMs, eliminating native operational overhead. The future of autonomous systems isn't just agents that code faster—it's agents that prove their work before hitting production. Further Reading Primary Source: Simmis: Lean on the JVM: proved functions for Java and Clojure SDK Artifact: org.replikativ/ansatz-lean-jvm on Clojars Lean 4 Guide: Functional Programming in Lean by David Thrane Christiansen"}, :excerpts [{:text "... self-correct via Lean compiler proofs, and deploy proved code into a Clojure service. Credit &...", :matched-terms ["deploy"], :source :content} {:text "... Wall in Production Every engineering team deploying autonomous AI coding agents eventually runs...", :matched-terms ["deploy"], :source :content} {:text "... Cycles across the boundary leak memory. Deployed servers required the full native Lean toolchain...", :matched-terms ["deploy"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-11-06T06:02:21.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-11-06T06:02:21.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rails", :item {:feed-id "sean-goedecke", :item-id "https://seangoedecke.com/a-database-is-a-store-for-global-variables/", :title "A database is a store for global variables", :link "https://seangoedecke.com/a-database-is-a-store-for-global-variables/", :published-at #inst "2026-11-06T00:00:00.000-00:00", :content "Databases are for storing global variables. We usually call them “rows” or “records” instead, but they’re definitionally global variables: any part of your program can read or write them. Isn’t this kind of weird? It’s common programming advice that global variables are bad, and yet many elegant programs rely on databases. Either the common advice is wrong, or there’s something special about databases that makes them the exception to the rule. What’s so bad about global variables? Here’s the two main1 reasons, in my view: They allow any part of your program to implicitly send data to any other part instead of passing it around via function signatures They couple different parts of your program: if one part depends on a variable having some value, any other part can break it Both of these apply to databases. Any part of your program that makes a query can send and receive data from any other part of your program. In practice this happens all the time: one function will save some data on a record, and another one will check for it. And if you haven’t seen bugs where some piece of code makes some incorrect expectation about the shape2 of a database column, you haven’t been working on web systems for long enough. So why aren’t database-backed apps in terrible architectural shape? In the rest of this post I’ll consider some options. First, we could bite the bullet: maybe all database-backed apps really are a mess. This is surprisingly plausible. When I think about the systems I’ve worked on that haven’t involved a database, they’re usually much cleaner and more elegant. If managing state is the hardest thing about designing software — and I think it is — then systems that manage less state are going to be better-designed. However, this doesn’t explain everything. Database-backed apps aren’t that bad, and even in database-backed apps, we try to avoid introducing regular global variables3, so in practice we treat global variables as worse. Second, we could say that databases offer ACID guarantees that mitigate the damage of global variables. Unlike regular global variables, databases offer a consistent view of state and the ability to atomically change multiple pieces of state together. I don’t really buy this. The problem with global variables is not just that they can get out of sync, it’s that they exist at all. If you think this is the difference, you are committed to thinking4 that if you stick a mutex around a global variable then it suddenly becomes acceptable. Third, we could say that databases are more explicit. Mutating or reading a global variable looks like ordinary code, but database queries need some connection object to be passed in and some query function to be invoked. When you’re scanning through code, you can see where the database queries are at a glance5. Not so for global variables. This does actually kind of mitigate the implicit-coupling problem. Maybe regular global variables would be better if you had to set and get them with long, involved function calls. Fourth, we could say that databases are less flexible. A global variable could store anything: a function, a class, an image, and so on. A database just stores data. I can buy this explanation for untyped languages. Global Ruby variables that could be reassigned from a string to a hash table are far more terrifying than a “global” database column that could change from one string to another string. But since the advice to avoid global variables applies to typed languages as well, I don’t think this could be the reason why databases are better. Fifth, we could say that we know how to use databases. For instance, whenever I read from a database, I reflexively check the type of the column so that my code doesn’t make assumptions that aren’t guaranteed by the database. There are use-cases that I would never even consider using a database for (e.g. to track a counter in a loop, or to store the name of the .env file where my config is stored). In other words, we have norms and customs6 around using databases that minimize a lot of the damage that global variables can cause. There’s nothing inherent about databases that make them better, it’s just how we use them in practice. Overall I think the third and fifth reasons are most convincing: databases are better because they’re more explicit and because we only tend to use them for a limited set of purposes. It’s interesting to note that these reasons are contingent, not inherent. A codebase which had conventions around global variable use7 could also fulfil these conditions. Would this make global variables fine, or at least no worse than a database? I think so. I don’t recommend you go out and start putting global variables in your programs. It’s more interesting to note the meta-point here: that two pieces of common software engineering dogma — avoid global variables and use a database for state — are partially inconsistent. Most software design knowledge is still just a pile of loose heuristics. I don’t really know how to build software and you don’t either. As I’ve written about before, we should therefore all be very suspicious of engineers who appeal to “best practice” without being able to give concrete reasons, even in cases as obvious as “global variables are bad”. For instance, concerns about readability and tracing follow from the first reason. I don’t really buy reasons like “it’s not thread-safe” or “global variables might shadow local variables”: any architectural problem that can be trivially solved with a mutex or a linter is not a real problem. ↩ For instance, that a string supports 4-byte UTF-8, or that a column can/can’t be NULL. ↩ You could say we avoid introducing global variables because databases are essential and global variables are not. In theory you could replace any global variable by threading a local variable through, but you do need a database if you’ve got gigabytes of structured data to manage. However, that doesn’t explain why database-backed apps still tend to be better-designed than apps with many global variables. ↩ I’m not saying this is necessarily wrong. The argument would go something like “well, if you implement a simple in-memory database for your global variables, that is better!” ↩ This is only sort of true. If you’re designing a system, you’d be well-advised to make it as true as you can. While I love Rails, my main beef with ActiveRecord is that it makes queries invisible. ↩ To be fair, some of these are guaranteed by the fact that the database exists over the network. ↩ Plus types, probably, and concurrency controls if we’re not in a single-threaded language. ↩"}, :excerpts [{:text "... And if you haven’t seen bugs where some piece of code makes some incorrect expectation about the shape2...", :matched-terms ["code"], :source :content} {:text "... or reading a global variable looks like ordinary code, but database queries need some connection object...", :matched-terms ["code"], :source :content} {:text "... to be invoked. When you’re scanning through code, you can see where the database queries are at a...", :matched-terms ["code"], :source :content}]}]